    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2410_14257_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "E2E latency as: = 2 , (4) where 2 is the threshold of end-to-end latency. Obviously, if the last token is generated before the end-to-end latency, the request meets the SLO. As aforementioned, the end-to-end latency and TPOT is a very loose constraint, which is not aligned with user experience all the time. Obviously, the TPOT can be seen as a generalization of E2E latency, therefore, we will not discuss the E2E latency in the following sections. 3.2 Counterintuitive Example of Existing SLOs We " .

