    kg:textSpan "words/min) [ 58 ] afterward. Apart from normalized latency 1 [18, 53], this serving goal is also measured by two metrics: time to first token (TTFT) and time per output token (TPOT) [ 58 ]. We refer to such LLM workload as real-time or RT requests. Offline applications, such as the asynchronous document processing service provided by OpenAIs Batch API [ 28 ], usually involve inference workloads processed in a batch manner within a specified time window (e.g., 24 hours in OpenAIs Batch API). Ther" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_09590_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.09590_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2504.09590_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_09590_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "requests to avoid starvation. The proposed heuristic first batches RT requests according to their dynamically assigned priorities and then adaptively replaces low-priority RT requests with BE requests. We further provide an SLO-aware adaptive batch sizing mechanism to control the runtime batch size. As a result, BROS strikes a favorable serving trade-off between RT and BE requests. We resolve memory competition between RT and BE requests with a bidirectional storage layout design for the KV cach" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_09590_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.09590_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2504.09590_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_09590_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "are insensitive to latency. Moreover, BE and RT requests exhibit conflicting preferences for system configurations, particularly in batch sizing. RT requests favor smaller batches to minimize latency [ 5 , 26 , 44 ], while BE requests benefit from larger batches to maximize GPU utilization and throughput [ 18 , 53 ]. These opposing requirements create significant challenges in configuring dynamic RT/BE co-serving systems. Further , co-scheduling RT/BE requests brings GPU memory contention associ" .

