    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2512_03416_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "and decoders. From the pearson correlation coefficient, we can see TokenScale achieves the highest correlation, 0 . 63 for prefillers and 0 . 44 for decoders, which indicates that its scaling policy most closely follows the ideal provisioning trend among all baselines. DistServe is the second-best system, as it uses an LLM simulator to determine its scaling thresholds. In contrast, both AIBrix and BlitzScale exhibit significant fluctuations in the number of prefiller instances. This behavior ari" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2512_03416_rag__chunk_019> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2512.03416_rag::chunk_019" ;
    kg:docId "2512.03416_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2512_03416_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "burst workloads of LLMs without relying on autoscaling. Some methods rely on SLO-aware routing strategies that drop requests when necessary [8], [48] while others [13], [20], [48], [39] overprovision resources to mitigate performance degradation. Works like [22], [36] adjusts model and KV-Cache (KVC) configurations to react to traffic changes. In contrast, TokenScale handles bursts by timely and accurate scaling, obviating the need for dropping requests, overprovisioning and heavy modification t" .

