    kg:docId "2405.19213_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2405_19213_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "latency if the model is not loaded ( cold inference ). Moreover, real-world workloads are highly dynamic [ 53 , 78 ], and it is difficult to predict the workloads accurately. Incorrect estimation of the demands for specific models can lead to significant runtime GPU resource scaling and allocation issues. On the other end, failing to provide a sufficient number of models to express the spectrum causes a lack of granularity in model selection, which results in using overkilled models to address s" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2409_15104_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2409.15104_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2409.15104_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2409_15104_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "27 ] largely ignore this heterogeneity between short-input and long-input requests. Cluster-level scheduling in current LLM inference systems generally falls into three categories: First-In-First-Out (FIFO) [ 19 , 23 ], reservation-based [ 24 ], and priority-based [ 25 27 ] strategies. However, these works primarily target short-input requests with input lengths below 2K. In clusters with both short-input and long-input requests, FIFO [ 19 , 23 ] can cause head-of-line blocking, where long input" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2411_00915_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2411.00915_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2411.00915_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2411_00915_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "inference yields up to 140ms additional latency when serving four 1024-token requests. This unnecessary waste is sufficient for the base model to perform 4x256 inference, with resources to spare! The reason for such a high cost is two-fold. 1) Inherently, additional overhead stems from two additional matrix multiplications and one additional matrix addition per layer as depicted in Fig. 2(a); meanwhile, these computations run in parallel with the base model computations, each layer requires addi" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2501_06709_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2501.06709_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2501.06709_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2501_06709_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "system that can be used by the request migration without affecting the normal operation of the system. Next, it migrates each request by transferring either tokens or KV cache, and orchestrates all requests to be migrated to make the consumption within the boundary. As shown in Figure 9, its workflow consists of the following steps. Boundary Profiling. We first define the communication boundary of a GPU communication link as a certain amount of data that can be transferred over the link. This bo" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2501_06709_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2501.06709_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2501.06709_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2501_06709_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to update the allocation based on three operations: (1) allocating new requests to the most appropriate GPU, (2) dropping completed requests to free resources, and (3) updating the position of running requests along with their processing. Details of each operation are given in Figure 10, which guarantees that our algorithm maintains a near-optimal number of GPUs, as proven in VII. Request Operation Batching. The request operations in Figure 10 are designed to efficiently manage individual reques" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2501_14312_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2501.14312_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "2501.14312_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2501_14312_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "related to fairness in LLM serving ( 3 ), then we introduce our fair scheduling design for a single worker ( 4 ), and finally, we expand this approach to distributed fair scheduling ( 5 ). even when the output length of the request is unknown. 3 Preliminaries 2.2 The Trade-offs In this section, we first formally define the properties a fair scheduling algorithm needs to meet for LLM serving, following those described in VTC [ 42 ]. We then discuss the cost function we adopt for service measureme" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2502_12574_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
