    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2503_18292_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "(c) Memory allocated for layer cross.1 Figure 7: Memory layout of PagedAttention and J ENGA A naive allocation strategy can lead to large internal fragmentation due to the different allocation and free patterns in LLM workloads. As shown in Figure 8a , memory allocation for a single request is often interleaved with allocations for other requests, whereas memory deallocation for a single request typically occurs together. When a request is completed, numerous small pages will be freed. However, " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_11750_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.11750_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2504.11750_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_11750_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "discusses the background topics related to this work, including emerging latency-sensitive inference workloads, different CPU-GPU coupling paradigms, kernel fusion techniques, current profiling tools, and comparisons with prior related works. A. LLM Workloads and Emerging Datacenter Applications AI inference costs increasingly exceed training costs, with potentially up to 90% AI-related costs attributed to inference due to its repeated execution across millions of user requests in pervasive AI s" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_11816_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.11816_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2504.11816_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_11816_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "for LLM inference in cloud environments. By leveraging InferSave , users can easily find the most cost-effective VM instance that meets their specified SLO while minimizing operational expenses. Experimental results show that applying InferSave achieves significant cost savings compared to traditional maximum-performance-based policies, with reductions of up to 73.7% for online workloads and 20.19% for offline workloads. In addition, it is designed to be flexible across various AWS instances and" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_15299_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.15299_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2504.15299_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_15299_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to avoid storing multiple versions of different bit-width. Challenge #3. Significant runtime overhead on weight dequantization operations. During model inference, the weight-only quantization approach requires the online transformation of quantized weights into the same data type as the activation for matrix computation. This dequantization operation introduces significant runtime overhead, typically accounting for 20%-70% of the entire inference process. To minimize the time spent on dequantiza" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2504_20828_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2504.20828_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2504.20828_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2504_20828_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "based on their primary design goals: prefill-prioritizing , decode-prioritizing , and disaggregationbased strategies. LLM inference servers such as vLLM [ 8 ] and Orca [ 26 ] adopt a prefill-prioritizing strategy that emphasizes responsiveness by ensuring the generation of the first token, thereby minimizing TTFT. However, frequently interrupting ongoing decode operations to serve new prefill requests can lead to generation stalls [1], potentially degrading throughput. At the other end of the sp" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2505_14468_rag__chunk_014> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2505.14468_rag::chunk_014" ;
    kg:docId "2505.14468_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2505_14468_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "of loading each artifact, especially the backbone LLM. While InstaInfer and ServerlessLLM introduces higher cumulative cold-start latency than inference, indicating their limitations in serving LoRA LLMs. InstaInfer ServerlessLLM ServerlessLoRA dLoRA vLLM 8 8 8 Normal Predictable Bursty 7 7 7 6 6 6 5 5 5 4 4 4 3 3 3 2 2 2 1 1 1 0 0 0 13B Series 13B Series 13B Series 7B Series 7B Series 7B Series Figure 9. Cost-effectiveness of ServerlessLoRA and baselines running the Predictable, Normal, and Bur" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_09019_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.09019_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2507.09019_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_09019_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "metrics to characterize inference performance, and an overview of unique properties of LLMs inference systems that makes their evaluation challenging. 2.1 LLM Inference Process There are two distinct phases in LLM inference a prefill phase followed by a decode phase. During prefill phase, the users input prompt is processed and first output token is produced. Next, during the decode phase, output tokens are generated one at a time, where the token generated in one step is passed through the mode" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_11507_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.11507_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2507.11507_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_11507_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Oneiros: KV Cache Optimization through Parameter Remapping for Multi-tenant LLM Serving Ruihao Li 1 , Shagnik Pal 1 , Vineeth Narayan Pullu 1 Prasoon Sinha 1 Jeeho Ryoo 2 Lizy K. John 1 Neeraja J. Yadwadkar 1 1 The University of Texas at Austin 2 Fairleigh Dickinson University Abstract memory a major bottleneck for efficient inference serving [ 11 , 18 , 27 , 39 , 41 , 42 , 66 ]. KV cache [ 27 , 49 ] was introduced as a key mechanism to reduce inference latency: it stores intermediate results fr" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2508_15919_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2508.15919_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2508.15919_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2508_15919_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "cannot specify explicit metrics such as TTFT or TPOT. In real deployments, customers often lack precise latency targets, but they can usually express the relative importance of their applications. Our scheme leverages these priorities to derive latency SLOs dynamically, adapting them over time as the system operates. This is illustrated in Figure 2 and explained below with five steps. (1) Priority as input. Each request is assigned a priority , with 0 being the highest and 1 the lowest, with rep" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2508_16646_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2508.16646_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2508.16646_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2508_16646_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan ", 46 ], leaving fair 1 drives this conflict because prefill processes prompts in parallel with superlinear compute-bound costs, while decode generates tokens sequentially with cache-dominated memorybound costs [ 49 ]. Equal token counts thus yield vastly different resource consumption. As Figure 1 demonstrates, identical token counts produce divergent outcomes across latency , throughput , and GPU utilization due to opposing computational constraints. Latency grows monotonically as a memory-boun" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2508_19559_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2508.19559_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2508.19559_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2508_19559_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "P/D Disaggregated Serving coordination mechanisms to manage interdependent components like prefill and decode pools, which must be scaled in a balanced manner. This coordination gap creates substantial operational overhead, a challenge that becomes even more severe for disaggregated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) serving [ 58 ]. HeteroScales design is tailored to the specific challenges of this environment, which is characterized by three key features: LLM inference consists of two distinct phasespref" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2509_16495_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2509.16495_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2509.16495_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2509_16495_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "documents can cause high-traffic bursts that require high combined throughput of input and output tokens to minimize the cost per token. In enterprise systems, the request traffic pattern is often mixed and dynamically changes over time in unpredictable ways. In such dynamic settings, it is a challenge to optimize for different traffic patterns simultaneously, since existing parallelization techniques impose significant trade-offs. 1 Introduction LLM inference has become the dominant workload in" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2510_11938_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2510.11938_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2510.11938_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2510_11938_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "InfiniBand) for efficient tensor parallelism. The result is a paradoxical scenario: individual GPUs are abundant, but cohesive GPU clusters are scarce, fundamentally undermining communicationintensive parallelism strategies. The serverless resource allocation model operates through dual-tier provisioning: always-on resources (60-75% of peak capacity) guarantee baseline service levels, while elastic resources handle demand spikes. This conservative approach, designed to prevent service outages, c" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2511_04791_rag__chunk_004> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2511.04791_rag::chunk_004" ;
    kg:docId "2511.04791_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2511_04791_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to support multi-tenant execution. Several mechanisms have been developed to enable such partitioning. Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) ( NVIDIA , 2025d ) statically divides a GPU into multiple isolated instances, each with dedicated compute and memory resources. Multi-Process Service (MPS) ( NVIDIA , 2025e ) supports coarse-grained spatial sharing by assigning fixed groups of SMs to different processes. However, these mechanisms cannot be applied within a single process, and the overhead of reconfiguri" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2511_06724_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2511.06724_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2511.06724_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2511_06724_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "smaller/faster models ( SM )there is no single winner under all circumstances. In 2, we highlighted that batching does not improve throughput for T2I model serving. T2I models are highly compute-bound. In Fig. 14, we compare speed-ups across batch sizes for non- DMs such as YOLOv5n [ 64 ], ResNet50 [ 41 ], E$cientNet-b4 [ 71 ], and GPT-8B [ 28 ], alongside various DMs . Notably, DMs show signi!cantly slower speed-ups that plateau rapidly, highlighting their limited ability to bene!t from batchin" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2511_21862_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2511.21862_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2511.21862_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2511_21862_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "Flash Attention or similar fused kernels, intermediate attention-score tensors are stored in on-chip SRAM using tiling, avoiding excessive memory traffic for long sequences. We therefore model the fused attention kernel as a single operator, which better captures its actual runtime behavior. = ( , ) (1) 6 Moreover, and denote the static runtime overhead latency of a Prefill and a Decode iteration, respectively. This includes CPU-side logic, kernel launch time, and other fixed costs. Such overhea" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/906419cd502575b617cc489a1a696a67-Paper-Conference_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "906419cd502575b617cc489a1a696a67-Paper-Conference_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "906419cd502575b617cc489a1a696a67-Paper-Conference_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/906419cd502575b617cc489a1a696a67-Paper-Conference_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "memory capacities of GPUs, they are often underutilized while decoding. Consequently, increasing the batch size can lead to higher throughput. A direct approach to reducing the number of adapters in a running batch is to prioritize batching requests that use the same adapter, a strategy we term adapter clustering. However, adapter clustering comes with its own set of trade-offs. For example, it can hurt the average latency or fairness among adapters. We provide an ablation study in Appendix A to" .

